<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daorson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th–1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daorson was the fortified hilltop centre of the Illyrian Daorsi at Ošanići, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stolac">Stolac</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>. Its acropolis was enclosed by massive walls of large stone blocks, often compared with Mycenaean Cyclopean masonry, and finds include coins, helmets and Greek-style pottery that show contact with the Greek Adriatic. The settlement flourished from the 4th century BCE and was destroyed in the mid-1st century BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daorson.jpg" alt="Daorson" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prof saxx at Croatian Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daorson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Daorsi lived in the valley of the Neretva River, and their capital stood on a ridge whose remains fall into three linked groups shaped by the lie of the land. A dominant hill fort or acropolis occupies the centre, with terraces below it to the south and south-west. To the east, on the Banje plateau, lay the outer settlement with its residential, artisan and trade quarters. The defensive wall running from south-west to north-east was 65 metres long, 4.2 metres wide and from 4.5 to 7.5 metres high, with doors and towers on both sides.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The hill fort was built over a prehistoric fortified settlement that existed from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, in the 17th or 16th century BCE, to the end of the late Bronze Age. The megalithic wall dates from the 4th century BCE, and the rest of the acropolis is later. The Daorsi met Greek traders early and the town took on a degree of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenization">Hellenization</a>. When the Delmatae attacked them, they joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issa-dalmatia">Issa</a> in seeking Roman protection, abandoned Caravantius and fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with their strong navy, and after the Illyrian Wars Rome granted them immunity.</p><p>After the peace treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 168 or 167 BCE the Daorsi minted their own coins. A small building held a mint, where thirty-nine coins were found, 29 of them depicting King Ballaios, who ruled after 168 BCE. Other finds include wine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a>, decorated buckles, graffiti on pottery, parts of stone statues some 2 metres tall and a sculpture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmus">Cadmus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harmonia">Harmonia</a> with an Illyrian relief of thirteen snakes. A bronze helmet carries the Greek inscription ΠΙИ, probably the abbreviated name of its owner, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinnes">Pinnes</a>. The town's end can be dated from the wars of the Roman praetor Vatinius against the Delmatae, and no permanent settlement ever rose on its ruins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Daorson is a National monument of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. With the nearby Radimlja necropolis and other sites around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stolac">Stolac</a>, it forms the natural and architectural ensemble of Stolac, which has been placed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Tentative list for World Heritage status.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daorson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the 3rd century BCE they had a fortified centre at <b>Daorson</b>, on a hill near modern Stolac, enclosed by massive Cyclopean walls.</blockquote><a href="/state/daorsi">Daorsi</a></li></ul></section>
